  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA very blustery day across the region with winds gusting\n20-35mph behind the cold front that pushed through this morning.\nMost of the rain showers have moved east, but a few pop up lake\neffect showers will be possible into the evening for areas north\nof the Southern Tier. Model guidance did not do a great job\nadvertising these winds, as most models topped out around 25 mph\nor so over the past couple of days. These stronger winds are\nmixing down some very dry air above the surface, with dewpoints\nfalling into the 30s across much of the area and even a few 30 \ndegree readings across the northern Finger Lakes. Temps will top\nout in the upper 50s to low 60s.\n\nThe main forecast question for tonight is will we have\nwidespread frost formation with temperatures falling into the\nmid to upper 30s across the entire area. Winds are forecast to\nremain active through much of the night with NW flow across the\nregion. A high pressure system slowly moving eastward this\nevening will be the main feature that would allow frost to \ndevelop or not. If the high progresses eastward faster than\nexpected, winds will decouple quicker and allow for radiational\ncooling to take effect faster, bringing frost across much of the\narea. A slower progression eastward keeps winds active and\nlimits frost to sheltered locations. Current thinking is winds\nwill lighten up enough over Bradford, Wyoming and Susquehanna\ncounties(currently in the growing season) that we will have a\nfew hours late tonight where we could get widespread frost\nformation. Because of this, a Frost Advisory has been issued\nfrom 1am to 9am. \n\nOur other growing zones; Yates, Seneca, Southern Cayuga and\nOnondaga counties in NY should miss out on much of the frost\nwith NW winds expected to remain active through the night. These\nNW winds could also move slightly warmer air off the warm waters\nof the Finger Lakes and Lake America into surrounding areas,\nhelping mitigate frost. If the high pressure system moves in a\nlittle quicker, we could see some patchy frost develop across\nsheltered areas. If your location frosts easily, take the\nproper precautions with outdoor plants.\n\nHigh pressure will dominate the area Tuesday, with temperatures\nin the 50s and continued NW winds and mostly sunny skies. The\nridge axis is currently modeled to move east of us by the\nevening hours, allowing southerly flow to return in the evening\nhours. The cold air will remain entrenched for one more night\nmostly east of I-81 with widespread mid to upper 30s expected. \nTo the west of I-81 onto the Wyoming Valley, WAA should occur\nthrough most of the night, keeping temperatures here in the low\n40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nFairly quiet weather is expected from mid-week into the weekend.\nA weak trough looks to slide through Ontario Wednesday into\nThursday, allowing for a chance for some light scattered rain\nshowers mostly across the northern portion of the area Wed night\nand Thursday. Temps will be warm Wed and Thurs with the\nsoutherly flow, topping out in the mid 60s to low 70s. A cold\nfront associated with the aforementioned trough will bring\ncooler temps in the upper 50s to mid 60s Fri and Sat with\nconditions remaining dry.",
